      "mentions": 24,
      "title": {
        "en": "Compassionate rescue for native wildlife"
      },
      "text": {
        "en": "Members of the public frequently highlight the center's dedication to saving injured or orphaned Texas wildlife, including songbirds, ducklings, squirrels, raccoons, and turtles. Reviewers feel reassured that creatures receive specialized trauma care before eventual release back into nature."
      }
    },
    {
      "topic": "place",
      "sentiment": "positive",
      "mentions": 9,
      "title": {
        "en": "Clean, welcoming wildlife facility"
      },
      "text": {
        "en": "Visitors consistently comment on the modern, organized, and clean environment of the campus. Many describe easy parking, well-maintained landscaped grounds, and comfortable drop-off spaces that provide a calm atmosphere for both rescuer families and animals in distress."
      }
    },
    {
      "topic": "staff",
      "sentiment": "positive",
      "mentions": 9,
      "title": {
        "en": "Helpful intake workers and volunteers"
      },
      "text": {
        "en": "Many callers and visitors appreciate the friendly staff and volunteers who explain what will happen to rescued animals and provide telephone guidance. A small minority of visitors felt individual intake workers seemed hurried or less empathetic when receiving found creatures."
      }
    }
  ],
  "faq": [
    {
      "q": {
        "en": "What kinds of animals does the wildlife center accept?"
      },
      "a": {
        "en": "The facility accepts injured, sick, and orphaned native Texas wildlife, including birds, squirrels, raccoons, opossums, and turtles."
      },
      "basis": "both"
    },
    {
      "q": {
        "en": "Is there a fee to surrender an injured wild animal?"
      },
      "a": {
        "en": "No, the center accepts wildlife free of charge, though voluntary donations are encouraged to help fund rehabilitation care."
      },
      "basis": "both"
    },
    {
      "q": {
        "en": "How long does the animal intake drop-off process take?"
      },
      "a": {
        "en": "Reviewers report that drop-off is straightforward and typically takes about ten to fifteen minutes from arrival."
      },
      "basis": "reviews"
    },
    {
      "q": {
        "en": "Can visitors volunteer at the wildlife center?"
      },
      "a": {
        "en": "Yes, community members note positive experiences volunteering across various roles on the Houston SPCA campus."
      },
      "basis": "both"
    }
